---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const TOPIC_ORDER = [
  "foundations", "paradigms", "inductive", "features",
  "biasvariance", "vc", "prep", "linreg", "gd", "prob"
];
const TOPIC_META = {
  foundations: { title: "What Is Machine Learning?", blurb: "Definitions, why rule-based systems break down, and where ML actually earns its keep." },
  paradigms:   { title: "Supervised, Unsupervised & Reinforcement Learning", blurb: "Telling the paradigms apart by what experience the learner gets, not by the algorithm." },
  inductive:   { title: "Inductive Learning, Hypothesis Spaces & Bias", blurb: "Why learning at all requires an assumption, and what kind of assumption an algorithm is making." },
  features:    { title: "Features & Feature Space", blurb: "Turning raw data into the coordinates an algorithm can actually draw a boundary in." },
  biasvariance:{ title: "Bias, Variance & Generalization", blurb: "Underfitting vs. overfitting, Ockham's razor, and why you can't drive both errors to zero at once." },
  vc:          { title: "Shattering & VC-Dimension", blurb: "A formal way to talk about how expressive a hypothesis class is." },
  prep:        { title: "Preparing Data for ML", blurb: "Cleaning, missing values, and why feature scale quietly breaks distance-based and gradient-based methods." },
  linreg:      { title: "Linear Regression", blurb: "Simple and multiple regression, the least-squares objective, and reading the error surface." },
  gd:          { title: "Gradient Descent Variants", blurb: "Batch, stochastic and mini-batch descent — the tradeoffs that actually show up in training logs." },
  prob:        { title: "Probability Foundations", blurb: "Sample spaces, events, axioms, and the machinery every probabilistic ML model sits on top of." }
};

const QUESTIONS = [
{ topic:"foundations", q:"Tom Mitchell's 1998 definition says a program learns from experience E with respect to task T and performance measure P if its performance at T, measured by P, improves with E. For a spam filter, which of the following are correctly matched to E, T, or P?",
  options:[
    {t:"E = the collection of emails the user has already labeled spam/not-spam", c:true},
    {t:"T = classifying a new incoming email as spam or not spam", c:true},
    {t:"P = the fraction of new emails correctly classified", c:true},
    {t:"P = the total number of emails ever sent by anyone", c:false},
    {t:"T = the algorithm used to store emails on disk", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>The &lt;P,T,E&gt; triple is the standard way interviewers check you actually understand what 'learning' means operationally, not just as a buzzword. <b>E</b> is the data/interaction the system is exposed to (labeled emails). <b>T</b> is the task being performed at deployment time (classify a new email). <b>P</b> is a measurable score of how well T is being done (accuracy, F1, etc.) — it must be something you can compute, so 'total emails ever sent' isn't a performance measure of this system at all, and 'algorithm used to store emails' is an implementation detail, not the task.</p><p><b>Interview angle:</b> when asked to design an ML system, stating the &lt;P,T,E&gt; triple explicitly (and picking a P that's actually aligned with business goals, e.g. weighted for false-positive cost) is a strong opening move in a system-design round.</p>"
},

{ topic:"foundations", q:"Which statements correctly distinguish Arthur Samuel's (1959) definition of ML from Tom Mitchell's (1998) definition?",
  options:[
    {t:"Samuel's definition is informal and emphasizes 'not being explicitly programmed'", c:true},
    {t:"Mitchell's definition is formal and operational — it can be checked against a concrete P, T, E", c:true},
    {t:"Samuel's definition predates the use of neural networks for computer vision", c:true},
    {t:"Mitchell's definition requires the learner to use gradient descent", c:false},
    {t:"Samuel's definition was created for deep learning specifically", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>Samuel's phrase ('the ability to learn without being explicitly programmed') is a great intuition-builder but isn't falsifiable/testable on its own. Mitchell's &lt;P,T,E&gt; formulation is what lets you write down, for any proposed system, exactly what experiment would show it 'learned.' Neither definition ties ML to a specific algorithm family (gradient descent, neural nets, decision trees, etc.) — that's a category error interviewers listen for.</p>"
},

{ topic:"foundations", q:"A rule-based (hand-coded) system tries to recognize handwritten digits by measuring shape features like the number of closed loops. Based on the slide's OCR/digit examples, which statements are true?",
  options:[
    {t:"Handwriting varies enough between people that a single hand-crafted rule set will systematically misclassify some valid digits", c:true},
    {t:"Shape alone is not always sufficient — e.g. distinguishing a tiger from a zebra by silhouette alone fails for a white tiger", c:true},
    {t:"A purely local, character-by-character rule-based OCR system can misread 'football' as 'fooEball' because it ignores surrounding context", c:true},
    {t:"Rule-based systems always outperform learned systems once enough rules are added", c:false},
    {t:"The reason to prefer ML here is that we can't practically enumerate every visual variation by hand, so we let the system infer the pattern from labeled examples", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This is the core 'why ML' argument from the deck: for problems with huge natural variability (handwriting, animal appearance, language), enumerating explicit rules doesn't scale — you'd need an unbounded rule set to cover every case, and rules interact badly. The OCR example specifically shows that <i>local shape matching without context</i> produces plausible-looking but wrong output ('fooEball'), which is exactly why context-aware, learned models (even simple n-gram language models) outperform naive character-shape classifiers. No amount of manually added rules asymptotically beats a system that can generalize from data, because the rule-writer runs out of foresight before the data runs out of edge cases.</p>"
},

{ topic:"foundations", q:"According to the 'When is ML useful?' criteria discussed, which conditions should hold before reaching for a learned model instead of hand-written logic?",
  options:[
    {t:"A pattern genuinely exists in the data", c:true},
    {t:"The pattern can't be cheaply written down as an explicit closed-form rule or equation", c:true},
    {t:"You have (or can get) a sufficient amount of representative data — usually called out as the single most important condition", c:true},
    {t:"The label distribution must be perfectly balanced across all classes", c:false},
    {t:"The relationship must be linear", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This is a practical gate every ML engineer should run before proposing a model: if there's no real pattern, you'll just fit noise; if the pattern is trivially expressible as a formula (e.g. converting Celsius to Fahrenheit), a model is overkill and adds needless risk/latency/maintenance; and without enough representative data, even the right model architecture will fail to generalize. Class balance and linearity are neither necessary conditions — plenty of successful ML systems handle imbalance (with reweighting/resampling) and nonlinearity (via nonlinear models), so they don't gate the initial 'should we even use ML' decision.</p><p><b>Interview angle:</b> this is the mental checklist behind 'when would you NOT use a neural network' — a classic system-design probe.</p>"
},

{ topic:"foundations", q:"Which of the following correctly describe the relationship between AI, ML, and Deep Learning as nested fields?",
  options:[
    {t:"Machine Learning is a subset of Artificial Intelligence", c:true},
    {t:"Deep Learning is a subset of Machine Learning", c:true},
    {t:"Every AI system is necessarily a Machine Learning system", c:false},
    {t:"A rule-based expert system with no learned parameters can still count as AI", c:true},
    {t:"Deep Learning refers specifically to models built from multiple layers of learned representations (e.g. neural networks)", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>The classic nested-circles picture (AI ⊃ ML ⊃ DL) is a favorite whiteboard question. AI is the broadest field: any system that exhibits behavior we'd call 'intelligent,' including purely symbolic/rule-based planners and search algorithms with zero learning (e.g. A* pathfinding, minimax game trees) — so 'every AI system is ML' is false. ML narrows this to systems that improve via data/experience. DL further narrows ML to models composed of many layers of learned, hierarchical representations (CNNs, transformers, etc.).</p>"
},

{ topic:"foundations", q:"Which of these are cited as reasons for ML's recent surge in popularity, distinct from the core mathematical ideas (which are decades old)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Availability of GPUs and other specialized hardware", c:true},
    {t:"Cloud compute making large-scale training accessible without owning hardware", c:true},
    {t:"Availability of large labeled/unlabeled datasets", c:true},
    {t:"Mature implementation platforms/frameworks such as PyTorch and TensorFlow", c:true},
    {t:"The invention of the perceptron in 1957, which happened at the same time", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>A key interview distinction: the underlying theory (perceptrons 1957/58, backprop formalized in the 1980s, SVMs in the 1990s) is old. What changed circa 2010s was the surrounding ecosystem — GPU throughput for matrix math, elastic cloud compute, internet-scale datasets, and frameworks (PyTorch/TensorFlow) that made iterating on architectures fast for non-systems-experts. Knowing this story shows you understand ML's history isn't 'suddenly discovered math,' it's 'old math finally had the substrate to run at scale.'</p>"
},

{ topic:"foundations", q:"Traditional programming and machine learning are often contrasted as two different 'functions' a computer computes. Which statements correctly describe this contrast?",
  options:[
    {t:"Traditional programming: Data + Program → Output", c:true},
    {t:"Machine learning (training time): Data + Expected Output (labels) → Program (the learned model)", c:true},
    {t:"In ML, the 'program' produced by training is often itself just a set of numeric parameters plus a fixed computation graph", c:true},
    {t:"Traditional programming can never use conditional logic", c:false},
    {t:"ML removes the need for any data at inference/prediction time", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This flip is the single clearest way to explain ML to a non-technical stakeholder: instead of a human writing the transformation rules (the program) by hand, the computer infers the transformation from examples of correct input→output pairs, and the output of that training process (weights, split thresholds, coefficients, etc.) becomes the new 'program' used at inference time. At inference/serving time, you absolutely still need input data — 'no data needed' is wrong; only training-time labels become unnecessary at inference.</p>"
},

{ topic:"foundations", q:"Which of the following are legitimate reasons an organization chooses ML-based automation over a human-in-the-loop decision process, per the 'Why ML?' motivations discussed (automation, speed, observer independence)?",
  options:[
    {t:"A trained model can apply the same criteria consistently, removing rater-to-rater (observer) variability", c:true},
    {t:"A model can score thousands of items per second, which no team of human reviewers can match", c:true},
    {t:"Automating a decision removes the need for any accuracy evaluation going forward", c:false},
    {t:"A model deployed as a decision-support system can flag cases for human review rather than fully replacing humans", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>'Observer independence' means two different human raters (or the same rater on different days) often disagree — a calibrated model applies the exact same function every time, which is valuable in domains like radiology triage or content moderation where consistency itself has value. Speed/scale is the throughput argument. Critically, automating a decision does <i>not</i> remove the need for ongoing evaluation — in fact it raises the stakes, since silent model drift can now affect every decision at scale, so monitoring becomes more important, not less.</p>"
},

{ topic:"paradigms", q:"Which of the following are the three paradigms of machine learning explicitly covered in the slides?",
  options:[
    {t:"Supervised learning", c:true},
    {t:"Unsupervised learning", c:true},
    {t:"Reinforcement learning", c:true},
    {t:"Transfer learning", c:false},
    {t:"Federated learning", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>Supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement learning are the three foundational paradigms defined by <i>what kind of experience/feedback</i> the learner receives: labeled (data, label) pairs, unlabeled data only, or a sequence of actions with delayed rewards/penalties from an environment. Transfer learning and federated learning are real, important techniques, but they're orthogonal concerns (how you reuse a model, or how you train across decentralized data) that can apply <i>within</i> any of the three paradigms — they aren't a fourth co-equal paradigm in this taxonomy.</p>"
},

{ topic:"paradigms", q:"In supervised learning, which statements about the 'learner' vs. the 'reasoner' are correct?",
  options:[
    {t:"During training, the learner receives both data (X) and labels (Y) — this is the 'experience'", c:true},
    {t:"The output of training is a model, and the process is called training", c:true},
    {t:"At deployment, the reasoner receives only the data (X) and must predict the label", c:true},
    {t:"The reasoner is given the correct label as an input so it can double-check its own prediction", c:false},
    {t:"Background knowledge is always strictly required for supervised learning to work at all", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This learner→model→reasoner pipeline is worth internalizing precisely because it maps directly onto MLOps terminology: 'learner' = training job, 'model' = serialized artifact/checkpoint, 'reasoner' = inference/serving. At inference time the true label is exactly what's unknown — if you had it, there'd be nothing to predict; that's the whole point of a held-out test set: labels exist for evaluation, but the model never sees them as input. Background/prior knowledge is optional — many supervised learners (e.g. plain linear regression from a CSV) work with zero domain knowledge injected.</p>"
},

{ topic:"paradigms", q:"Which of these are correctly classified as classification tasks vs. regression tasks in supervised learning?",
  options:[
    {t:"Predicting whether a tumor is 'benign' or 'malignant' → classification", c:true},
    {t:"Predicting a patient's hemoglobin level in g% → regression", c:true},
    {t:"Predicting a house's sale price in rupees → regression", c:true},
    {t:"Predicting whether an image shows a foreground or background pixel → classification", c:true},
    {t:"Predicting a robot arm's continuous (x, y) joint coordinate → classification", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>The discriminator is the type of the target/label: <b>categorical</b> (a finite set of classes, possibly just 2 as in binary classification) → classification; <b>real-valued/continuous</b> → regression. A continuous 2D coordinate is regression (in fact multi-output regression), not classification, even though it 'looks' like it could be bucketed — unless you deliberately discretize the coordinate space into bins, at which point it becomes a classification problem by construction.</p>"
},

{ topic:"paradigms", q:"An unsupervised clustering algorithm is given thousands of unlabeled customer-purchase vectors and must group similar customers. Which statements are true about this setting?",
  options:[
    {t:"The learner receives only data, with no label attached to any example", c:true},
    {t:"There is no 'ground truth' class name the model is trying to match during training", c:true},
    {t:"A typical downstream use is building a recommendation system based on what similar customers previously bought", c:true},
    {t:"This is identical to supervised classification because both produce a category for each customer", c:false},
    {t:"Evaluating cluster quality can never be done without human-labeled ground truth", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>Clustering is unsupervised because no example carries a 'correct group id' during training — the algorithm discovers structure (cohesive groups of nearby points) rather than matching pre-assigned labels; that's fundamentally different from classification, where a known label set drives training. Cluster quality absolutely can be evaluated without ground truth using internal metrics like silhouette score or within-cluster sum of squares, even though external metrics (e.g. adjusted Rand index against known labels) are also an option when labels happen to exist for validation purposes only.</p>"
},

{ topic:"paradigms", q:"Which of the following are legitimate example applications of unsupervised learning mentioned in the deck (clustering, dimensionality reduction, association rule mining, generative models)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Recommendation systems based on grouping customers by past purchase behavior", c:true},
    {t:"Finding genetically similar viruses by clustering their genomic patterns", c:true},
    {t:"Compressing high-dimensional data by finding salient/most-informative features (dimensionality reduction)", c:true},
    {t:"Finding items that frequently co-occur in shopping baskets (association rule mining)", c:true},
    {t:"Predicting next month's exact revenue figure from last year's monthly revenue history", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>All four listed use-cases genuinely require no labels: clustering groups similar things, dimensionality reduction (e.g. PCA) finds a compact representation preserving structure, and association rule mining (e.g. market-basket analysis, 'customers who bought X also bought Y') finds co-occurrence patterns. Predicting a specific future numeric value from historical numeric values is textbook supervised regression — every historical (month, revenue) pair is itself a labeled training example, since 'last month's revenue' plays the role of a known target during training.</p>"
},

{ topic:"paradigms", q:"You are teaching a child to ride a bicycle. According to the reinforcement-learning framing used in the slides, which statements are correct?",
  options:[
    {t:"This is neither purely supervised nor purely unsupervised — it's driven by trial-and-error feedback", c:true},
    {t:"Falling down / losing balance acts as a penalty signal", c:true},
    {t:"Reaching the destination or receiving praise acts as a reward signal", c:true},
    {t:"What's being learned is a single, one-shot correct output — like a classification label", c:false},
    {t:"What's being learned is a sequence of good decisions (actions) that control an agent over time", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>Bike-riding is the deck's canonical intuition-builder for RL: there's no dataset of (situation, correct-handlebar-angle) pairs handed to the child up front (ruling out pure supervised learning), and it's not just 'find structure in static data' either (ruling out pure unsupervised learning) — instead, the learner tries actions, observes consequences (reward/penalty) from an environment, and adjusts a <i>policy</i> over a <i>sequence</i> of decisions, not a single-shot prediction. That sequential, feedback-driven, action-selection framing is exactly the Agent–Environment–State–Action–Reward loop formalized as a Markov Decision Process.</p>"
},

{ topic:"paradigms", q:"In the standard reinforcement-learning model (Agent, Environment, State S_t, Action A_t, Reward R_t), which statements are correct?",
  options:[
    {t:"The agent chooses an action based on the current state", c:true},
    {t:"The environment returns a reward and the next state after the agent's action", c:true},
    {t:"The agent's goal is typically to maximize cumulative reward over time, not just the immediate reward", c:true},
    {t:"The environment is optional and can be omitted for RL to work", c:false},
    {t:"The reward at time t+1, R_(t+1), can depend on the action taken at time t", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This is the standard RL loop: agent observes S_t, picks A_t, environment transitions and emits R_(t+1) and S_(t+1), and the loop repeats. The environment is not optional — it's the thing that actually defines the dynamics and reward function the agent is trying to learn about; without it there's nothing to interact with. And RL agents are explicitly optimizing for <i>long-run</i> reward (often formalized via a discounted sum), which is why a good RL policy sometimes accepts a worse immediate reward for a much better future payoff (e.g. sacrificing a chess piece for positional advantage).</p>"
},

{ topic:"paradigms", q:"Which of these are examples of 'other' learning paradigms mentioned (weakly-supervised, semi-supervised, self-supervised) beyond the three main ones?",
  options:[
    {t:"Semi-supervised learning: training with a small labeled set plus a much larger unlabeled set", c:true},
    {t:"Self-supervised learning: generating supervisory labels automatically from the data itself (e.g. predicting a masked word from its context)", c:true},
    {t:"Weakly-supervised learning: training with noisy, imprecise, or indirect labels rather than exact ground truth", c:true},
    {t:"Semi-supervised learning requires that no labels exist at all", c:false},
    {t:"Self-supervised learning is only usable for image data, never for text", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>These sit on the spectrum between fully supervised and fully unsupervised. Semi-supervised uses a small labeled + large unlabeled pool (common when labeling is expensive, e.g. medical imaging). Self-supervised constructs 'free' labels from the raw data's own structure — masked-language-model pretraining (BERT), next-token prediction (GPT), and image rotation/colorization pretext tasks are all self-supervised and span both text and vision (and audio, video, etc.). Weakly-supervised uses labels that are cheap but imperfect — e.g. labeling every pixel in an image with the image-level tag rather than doing precise pixel-level annotation.</p>"
},

{ topic:"paradigms", q:"Which of the following correctly distinguishes a classification model's target from a regression model's target, using the price-of-house and cat-vs-dog examples from the slides?",
  options:[
    {t:"'Cat' or 'Dog' is a categorical label → classification target", c:true},
    {t:"'1.7 Cr' (a price) is a real number → regression target", c:true},
    {t:"Both classification and regression can use the exact same input feature vector (e.g. height, weight) — only the target type differs", c:true},
    {t:"Classification always requires exactly two classes", c:false},
    {t:"Regression targets must always be positive numbers", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>The algorithm family (classification vs regression) is a property of the <i>target/label</i>, not the input features — the same feature vector (say, square footage + location + age of a house) could feed a regression model predicting price or a classification model predicting 'sells in under 30 days: yes/no.' Classification supports any number of classes ≥ 2 (multiclass, e.g. digit recognition 0–9), and regression targets can be negative (e.g. predicting temperature change, profit/loss) — 'always positive' is a dataset property, not a definitional requirement.</p>"
},

{ topic:"inductive", q:"In inductive learning for the supervised setting, we assume there exists a true target function f(X) and we try to learn a hypothesis h that approximates it. Which statements are correct?",
  options:[
    {t:"We never actually observe f directly — we only observe (X, Y) pairs sampled according to it", c:true},
    {t:"h is called a hypothesis", c:true},
    {t:"For classification, f(X) is categorical; for regression, f(X) is a real number; for density/probability estimation, f(X) is a probability", c:true},
    {t:"Inductive learning guarantees h = f exactly once enough data is seen", c:false},
    {t:"The set of all hypotheses a learning algorithm is willing to consider is called the hypothesis space", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This is the precise vocabulary interviewers expect: f is the unknown, unobservable ground-truth mapping; h is our approximation, drawn from a hypothesis space H (e.g. 'all straight lines,' 'all depth-3 decision trees'). Crucially, induction never <i>guarantees</i> h = f — inductive learning is fundamentally an ill-posed problem (more below) precisely because a finite training sample is consistent with infinitely many hypotheses, so we can only claim h approximates f well on the distribution the data came from, never that we've recovered f exactly.</p>"
},

{ topic:"inductive", q:"Why is inductive learning described as an 'ill-posed problem'?",
  options:[
    {t:"Because a finite set of training examples is consistent with many different hypotheses, and without seeing all possible examples we can't uniquely pin down one 'correct' hypothesis", c:true},
    {t:"Because we can never collect any training data at all", c:false},
    {t:"Because multiple different functions can perfectly fit the same finite training set yet disagree wildly on unseen inputs", c:true},
    {t:"Because the training loss can never be computed exactly", c:false},
    {t:"This is exactly why we must make an assumption — an inductive bias — to prefer one hypothesis over the others that also fit the data", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>'Ill-posed' here has a specific technical meaning: a well-posed problem has a unique solution determined by the given information; here, the given information (a finite labeled sample) is compatible with infinitely many candidate functions that all agree on the training points but diverge everywhere else. This is the formal justification for why every learning algorithm needs an inductive bias — some built-in preference (e.g. 'prefer simpler/smoother functions') that breaks the tie among all the hypotheses that fit the training data equally well.</p>"
},

{ topic:"inductive", q:"Consider a binary classification problem with N training points in a 2D feature space. Without any restriction on the hypothesis, how many distinct labelings (functions from points to {positive, negative}) are theoretically possible, and what does this imply?",
  options:[
    {t:"2^N distinct labelings are possible in the worst case", c:true},
    {t:"This number grows exponentially in N, so searching all of them becomes computationally infeasible for realistic N", c:true},
    {t:"This is exactly why we restrict ourselves to a smaller hypothesis space (e.g. only straight-line decision boundaries) rather than considering every conceivable labeling", c:true},
    {t:"This number is always exactly N regardless of how many points there are", c:false},
    {t:"Restricting the hypothesis space this way is called introducing an inductive bias", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>Each of the N points can independently be labeled one of 2 ways, giving 2^N possible label assignments — with even 30 points that's over a billion, so brute-force search over 'all conceivable functions' is a non-starter. This is precisely the practical motivation for restricting the hypothesis space to something tractable (straight lines, decision trees of bounded depth, neural nets of fixed architecture) — that restriction, plus any preference among the remaining hypotheses, together constitute the model's inductive bias.</p>"
},

{ topic:"inductive", q:"Which of the following correctly describe 'restriction bias' (a.k.a. absolute bias) as distinguished from 'preference bias' (a.k.a. relative bias)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Restriction bias defines the representational power of the algorithm — the entire set of hypotheses it is even capable of expressing", c:true},
    {t:"An algorithm restricted to straight-line decision boundaries has a restriction bias that excludes, say, circular decision boundaries entirely", c:true},
    {t:"Preference bias describes which hypotheses within an already-expressible hypothesis space the algorithm favors, e.g. preferring lines parallel to the x-axis", c:true},
    {t:"Restriction bias and preference bias are two names for exactly the same concept", c:false},
    {t:"L2 regularization (weight decay), which shrinks a neural network's weights toward zero without removing any architecture's expressiveness, is best understood as a form of preference bias", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This distinction shows up constantly in real system design: choosing a model <i>class</i> (linear model vs. gradient-boosted trees vs. deep net) sets your restriction bias — it hard-limits what functions are even reachable. Regularization, priors, or early stopping don't remove any hypothesis from being reachable in principle, they just make the optimizer prefer 'simpler' members of that same space — that's preference bias. Knowing which lever you're pulling (restriction vs. preference) is exactly how you'd explain, in an interview, why increasing model capacity vs. increasing regularization strength have different effects on the bias-variance tradeoff.</p>"
},

{ topic:"inductive", q:"Ockham's razor is invoked as a heuristic in model selection. Which statements about it are correct?",
  options:[
    {t:"It states we should prefer the simplest hypothesis that is still consistent with the training data", c:true},
    {t:"It is a genuine mathematical guarantee that the simplest model always generalizes best", c:false},
    {t:"The rationale is that nature often follows relatively simple underlying laws, so a simpler hypothesis has a better chance of matching the true process rather than just memorizing noise", c:true},
    {t:"It's connected to the 'No Free Lunch' idea that some inductive bias is unavoidable — Ockham's razor is simply one popular choice of bias", c:true},
    {t:"Preferring simpler hypotheses always means preferring a smaller number of trainable parameters, with no exceptions", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>Ockham's razor is a heuristic/assumption, not a theorem — the deck is explicit that 'this is just an assumption and does not always work.' It's useful precisely because it's one reasonable inductive bias among many, and the No Free Lunch theorem tells us that no bias is universally best across every possible problem — you're always betting that your assumption matches the world you're actually operating in. 'Simpler' is also not strictly synonymous with 'fewer parameters' — e.g. a smooth, low-curvature function can be 'simpler' in a meaningful sense even with many parameters, if those parameters are constrained (regularized) to behave smoothly.</p>"
},

{ topic:"inductive", q:"You fit a regression curve to 5 training points twice: once assuming the underlying relationship is 'current vs. voltage' (physically near-linear, Ohm's law) and once assuming it's 'day vs. stock price' (noisy and volatile). Which statements correctly reflect the lesson from this example?",
  options:[
    {t:"The best-fitting curve depends on our assumptions about the underlying process generating the data, not on the data points alone", c:true},
    {t:"For the current/voltage case, a simple straight line is a reasonable fit, with off-line points treated as measurement noise/outliers", c:true},
    {t:"For the volatile stock-price case, a highly wiggly curve that hits every point might be more 'honest' about the process, since real stock prices are noisy and non-smooth", c:true},
    {t:"There is one universally correct curve shape that fits any 5 data points regardless of what they represent", c:false},
    {t:"This example illustrates that choosing a hypothesis class is itself a modeling decision, not something the data can decide by itself", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This is the deck's most vivid illustration of inductive bias in action: identical (x, y) coordinate values call for different curve shapes depending on domain knowledge about the data-generating process. Ohm's law is genuinely near-linear physics, so a straight line with the odd measurement-noise outlier is the right story. Daily stock prices are driven by many volatile, hard-to-model factors, so a smooth low-degree curve would actually <i>underfit</i> the real dynamics — a rougher fit may better reflect reality. The lesson: 'best fit' is never data-only, it's data + assumptions about the domain.</p>"
},

{ topic:"inductive", q:"A car's speed-over-time plot could be drawn as either (a) a perfectly flat horizontal line or (b) a gently wavy curve with small ups and downs. Which statements correctly reflect the reasoning given in the slides?",
  options:[
    {t:"The wavy curve (b) is more realistic, since real driving involves traffic, signals, braking, and minor speed variation", c:true},
    {t:"The flat line (a) would only be accurate under many unrealistic assumptions — perfectly smooth road, zero traffic, no braking, no signals", c:true},
    {t:"A more realistic (less assumption-laden) model is described as 'more complex,' while a highly assumption-laden model is described as 'simpler'", c:true},
    {t:"More assumptions always correspond to lower bias in the statistical sense", c:false},
    {t:"'Simple curves require more assumptions, more bias' is the direct relationship drawn in this example", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This example is used to build intuition for a somewhat counter-intuitive equivalence: 'simpler model' = 'more restrictive assumptions' = 'higher bias.' A flat-line speed model is simple to write down, but only matches reality if you assume away all the real-world complexity (traffic, signals) — that's a strong, restrictive assumption, i.e. high bias. The wavy curve requires fewer such assumptions (it lets speed vary freely to match what actually happens) and is therefore lower-bias but more complex. This is exactly backwards from the naive intuition that 'more assumptions = more accurate,' which is why it's worth drilling.</p>"
},

{ topic:"vc", q:"What is meant by 'shattering' a set of points, and which statements about it are correct?",
  options:[
    {t:"A hypothesis space H shatters a set of N points if, for at least one arrangement of those N points, H contains a hypothesis matching every one of the 2^N possible positive/negative labelings", c:true},
    {t:"If a hypothesis space can shatter a set of points, it means that set's points can be given any conceivable label combination and some member of H will fit it perfectly", c:true},
    {t:"Shattering requires that H fits every possible arrangement of N points, not just one favorable arrangement", c:false},
    {t:"Shattering is a way of measuring how expressive/flexible a hypothesis space is", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>The definition only requires <i>one</i> favorable configuration of the N points to exist for which all 2^N labelings are achievable — it does not require every arrangement of N points to be shatterable. This subtlety is exactly what trips people up and is exactly what's tested by the '3 points, one bad arrangement vs. one good arrangement' example in the deck: straight lines can't shatter 3 collinear-ish points laid out one specific (unlucky) way, but they CAN shatter 3 points laid out a different way — and that single favorable arrangement is enough to say 'straight lines shatter 3 points.'</p>"
},

{ topic:"vc", q:"The deck shows that straight lines in a 2D plane can shatter 3 points (for a favorable arrangement) but cannot shatter any arrangement of 4 points. What is the correct conclusion, and why does the specific arrangement of 3 points matter?",
  options:[
    {t:"The VC-dimension of the hypothesis space of straight lines in 2D is 3", c:true},
    {t:"For some particular arrangements of exactly 3 points (e.g. one point positioned such that its label can't be flipped independently of the others by any line), straight lines fail to achieve all 8 labelings", c:true},
    {t:"But because at least one arrangement of 3 points IS fully shatterable, the VC-dimension is still 3, not 2", c:true},
    {t:"VC-dimension is defined using the worst-case (least favorable) arrangement of points, not the best case", c:false},
    {t:"Since no arrangement of 4 points can be shattered, VC-dimension caps at 3 for this hypothesis space", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This is one of the most commonly mis-stated definitions in ML interviews. VC-dimension is defined via a <b>best-case existential</b> quantifier over arrangements ('does THERE EXIST an arrangement of N points that can be shattered'), combined with 'and NO arrangement of N+1 points can be shattered.' So a hypothesis class can fail to shatter some unlucky arrangements of N points and still have VC-dimension ≥ N, as long as one lucky arrangement works. For 2D lines: some arrangement of 3 points is fully shatterable (max), but no arrangement of 4 points is (2^4=16 labelings; a line can only realize a subset, notably not the classic 'XOR-like' diagonal labeling), so VC-dim = 3.</p>"
},

{ topic:"vc", q:"What is the VC-dimension of the hypothesis class of axis-aligned rectangles in a 2D plane, and why?",
  options:[
    {t:"The VC-dimension is 4", c:true},
    {t:"4 points arranged in a diamond (one point extremal in each of the up/down/left/right directions) can realize all 16 possible +/- labelings using an axis-aligned rectangle", c:true},
    {t:"No arrangement of 5 points can be shattered by axis-aligned rectangles", c:true},
    {t:"Axis-aligned rectangles have infinite VC-dimension because a rectangle can be drawn arbitrarily large or small", c:false},
    {t:"Being able to draw infinitely many different rectangles automatically implies infinite VC-dimension", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This is a classic worked VC-dimension example precisely because it shows that having infinitely many candidate hypotheses (infinitely many rectangles by position/size) does NOT imply infinite VC-dimension — what matters is the maximum number of points whose label combinations can all be realized. With 4 points in a 'diamond' arrangement, you can independently include/exclude each extremal point via rectangle placement, hitting all 2^4=16 labelings; but with 5 points, by pigeonhole one point ends up strictly inside the convex hull of the others in every rectangle that contains them, so you can never label it negative while the surrounding points are positive. Hence VC-dim = 4.</p>"
},

{ topic:"vc", q:"According to the VC-dimension generalization bound shown in the slides (test error < training error + a complexity term that grows with D, the VC-dimension, and shrinks with N, the number of training samples), which statements are correct?",
  options:[
    {t:"Holding N fixed, increasing the VC-dimension D tends to loosen (worsen) the bound on test error, all else equal", c:true},
    {t:"The bound is stated as applicable specifically when D is much smaller than N (D ≪ N)", c:true},
    {t:"A higher VC-dimension means a more expressive/flexible model, which raises the risk of overfitting and hence a larger potential test-training error gap", c:true},
    {t:"This bound guarantees the test error will always be exactly equal to the training error", c:false},
    {t:"This kind of bound is a formal, theoretical justification for why 'more expressive models need proportionally more data to generalize reliably'", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This VC bound (from statistical learning theory / PAC learning) is the rigorous backbone behind the intuitive 'more complex models need more data' rule of thumb used throughout ML practice. It's a probabilistic upper bound, not an equality — it says test error is <i>likely</i> to be within training error plus a term that grows with model complexity (D) and shrinks with sample size (N). This is exactly the theoretical grounding for practical heuristics like 'don't use a 175B-parameter model on 500 rows' or 'if you scale up model capacity, scale up your training data too.'</p>"
},

{ topic:"inductive", q:"Which of the following are correctly described as inductive-bias decisions an ML engineer explicitly makes when designing a model, as opposed to something the data determines automatically?",
  options:[
    {t:"Choosing a linear model vs. a decision tree vs. a neural network", c:true},
    {t:"Choosing the maximum depth of a decision tree", c:true},
    {t:"Adding an L2 regularization penalty that shrinks weights toward zero", c:true},
    {t:"The actual numeric values the training data happens to contain", c:false},
    {t:"Choosing a convolutional architecture that assumes spatial locality/translation-invariance matters for image data", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>Everything in this list except the raw data values is a design choice that encodes an assumption about the world — model family (restriction bias), tree depth (restriction bias, controls the size of the reachable hypothesis space), L2 penalty (preference bias, favors small-weight solutions), and even architectural choices like convolution (restriction+preference bias baked in: 'nearby pixels matter more, and the same filter should apply everywhere in the image') are all inductive biases chosen by the practitioner. The raw data values themselves are just observations — they're what the bias gets applied <i>to</i>, not a form of inductive bias.</p>"
},

{ topic:"features", q:"Which statements correctly describe the relationship between raw data, features, and feature vectors, as introduced in the slides?",
