    {t:"Whether to log-transform is a decision that should be informed by understanding the feature's real-world distribution, not applied blindly to every numeric column", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This is a common real-world nuance beyond the raw standardization/min-max formulas: z-score standardization only recenters and rescales (shifts mean to 0, std to 1) — it's a purely linear transform, so a right-skewed distribution stays exactly as skewed after standardization, just shifted/rescaled. A log transform, by contrast, is nonlinear and specifically compresses large values proportionally more than small ones, which is why it's the standard first move for monetary amounts, population counts, and other naturally multiplicative/right-skewed quantities before any further linear scaling is applied.</p>"
},

{ topic:"prep", q:"Which statements correctly describe the risk of 'data leakage' during preprocessing, and how it connects to the train/validation/test split discipline?",
  options:[
    {t:"Computing normalization statistics (mean, std, min, max) using the full dataset (including validation/test rows) before splitting is a form of data leakage", c:true},
    {t:"Data leakage during preprocessing can make offline validation/test metrics look better than what the model will actually achieve on truly new production data", c:true},
    {t:"The correct order is: split the data first, then fit any preprocessing statistics only on the training partition, then apply those same statistics to transform validation/test partitions", c:true},
    {t:"Data leakage is impossible once you've started using a proper train/test split, regardless of when preprocessing statistics were computed", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This is one of the most practically important and most commonly violated rules in real pipelines — computing μ/σ or min/max (or target encodings, or imputation values) on the combined dataset before splitting lets information about the validation/test set's distribution 'leak' into the training-time transform, giving an overly optimistic offline metric that won't hold up once the model meets genuinely unseen production data. This is exactly why libraries like scikit-learn enforce a fit-on-train / transform-on-everything-else API pattern (fit_transform on train, transform only on val/test) — the API design itself is a defense against this specific, easy-to-make mistake.</p>"
},

{ topic:"linreg", q:"Which statements correctly describe multicollinearity in multiple linear regression, where two or more features are highly correlated with each other?",
  options:[
    {t:"Multicollinearity can make individual coefficient estimates unstable and hard to interpret, even if the model's overall predictions remain reasonably accurate", c:true},
    {t:"A small change in the training data can cause a disproportionately large swing in a correlated feature's estimated coefficient under multicollinearity", c:true},
    {t:"Ridge regression (L2-regularized linear regression) is a common technique used specifically to stabilize coefficients under multicollinearity", c:true},
    {t:"Multicollinearity always makes a linear regression model's predictions on new data worse", c:false}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This distinction — prediction accuracy vs. coefficient interpretability — is one that trips up a lot of people who only think of regression as a prediction tool: even with severe multicollinearity, a linear model can still predict well on new data drawn from the same distribution (since the <i>combined</i> effect of the correlated features is well-estimated even if it's ambiguous how to split credit between them individually), but trying to interpret 'feature A's coefficient means X' becomes unreliable, because the optimizer could have assigned that same combined effect many different ways across the correlated features. Ridge regression's L2 penalty specifically discourages any one coefficient from growing arbitrarily large to compensate for a correlated partner, which stabilizes the individual estimates.</p>"
},

{ topic:"gd", q:"Which statements correctly describe the relationship between the number of epochs, the learning rate schedule, and convergence in gradient-descent training, as commonly seen in practice?",
  options:[
    {t:"An epoch refers to one complete pass through the entire training dataset", c:true},
    {t:"A learning-rate schedule that decays η over time can help take large steps early (fast progress) and small steps later (fine convergence near the minimum)", c:true},
    {t:"Training for more epochs always strictly improves validation performance, with no risk of eventually overfitting", c:false},
    {t:"Monitoring the validation loss across epochs (not just training loss) is the standard way to decide when to stop training", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This links the mini-batch pseudocode's inner/outer loop structure to real training practice: one pass through all mini-batches = one epoch, and it's entirely standard to run many epochs. Learning-rate schedules (step decay, cosine annealing, warmup) exist precisely to get the best of both worlds — a large initial η for fast early progress across the broad part of the loss landscape, shrinking toward a small η for precise convergence near the minimum. And more epochs is explicitly NOT always better — past some point, additional epochs let the model keep fitting the training set's noise (exactly the overfitting story from the bias-variance section), which is why validation-loss monitoring (and early stopping) rather than a fixed epoch count is the standard practice.</p>"
},

{ topic:"gd", q:"Comparing plain (vanilla) gradient descent to momentum-based or adaptive-learning-rate variants (e.g. as used in modern deep learning), which statements are correct, based on the concepts introduced in this deck?",
  options:[
    {t:"Plain gradient descent, as covered here, always uses a single fixed learning rate η applied uniformly to every parameter at every step", c:true},
    {t:"The core update rule 'parameter ← parameter − η·gradient' is the foundational building block that momentum and adaptive methods (like Adam) extend, rather than replace outright", c:true},
    {t:"All gradient-based optimizers, however sophisticated, still fundamentally rely on computing ∂J/∂parameter at each step", c:true},
    {t:"Once you understand plain batch/stochastic/mini-batch gradient descent, you already have the conceptual foundation to make sense of why more advanced optimizers were invented", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This question deliberately looks forward from the deck's material to connect it with what a candidate would encounter next in a deep-learning-focused role: every modern optimizer (Momentum, RMSProp, Adam, AdamW) is still, at its core, computing gradients of a loss and using them to update parameters — what they add on top are smarter ways to set the effective step size per parameter and per iteration (e.g. accumulating a velocity term to smooth out noisy gradients, or adapting η per-parameter based on historical gradient magnitudes). Understanding plain batch/SGD/mini-batch deeply — including exactly why learning rate and feature scale matter — is precisely the prerequisite that makes those more advanced optimizers' design choices make sense rather than feel like arbitrary tricks.</p>"
},

{ topic:"prep", q:"A categorical feature 'city' has 500 distinct values, most appearing only once or twice in the training set. Which statements about preprocessing this feature are correct?",
  options:[
    {t:"Naive one-hot encoding would create 500 extremely sparse columns, most of which carry almost no statistical signal individually", c:true},
    {t:"Grouping rare categories into an 'other' bucket, or using target/frequency encoding, are common ways to handle high-cardinality categoricals", c:true},
    {t:"This kind of high-cardinality categorical column has no relationship at all to the earlier discussion of data quality and feature scale", c:false},
    {t:"Tree-based models can often handle high-cardinality categoricals somewhat more gracefully than distance-based models, but extreme cardinality can still hurt split quality and training time", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>High-cardinality categoricals are a very common real-world extension of the 'prepare your features properly' theme — this is a frequent live-coding/system-design topic at companies with lots of categorical business data (cities, product SKUs, merchant IDs). One-hot encoding 500 rare categories mostly produces near-constant, uninformative columns (curse-of-dimensionality-adjacent), which is why frequency encoding, target encoding (with proper cross-validation to avoid leakage), embedding layers (in deep models), or simply bucketing rare values into 'other' are the standard fixes.</p>"
},

{ topic:"gd", q:"Which statements correctly describe what happens to the least-squares linear regression objective's gradient as the model's parameters approach the true minimum?",
  options:[
    {t:"The gradient ∂J/∂w approaches zero as w approaches the minimizing value", c:true},
    {t:"Near the minimum, gradient descent's update steps naturally shrink in size, even with a constant learning rate η, because the step size is proportional to the gradient's magnitude", c:true},
    {t:"At the exact minimum of a convex objective like this one, the gradient is exactly zero", c:true},
    {t:"A nonzero gradient at a point always means that point cannot be a minimum of a convex function", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This is the calculus fact underlying the entire gradient-descent story: for a smooth convex function, the gradient vanishing is both necessary and sufficient to identify the (unique, global) minimum, which is exactly why 'set the gradient to zero and solve' is the closed-form approach for linear regression, and why gradient descent's steps naturally taper off as training converges rather than needing an explicit stopping rule based on step count alone — watching the gradient norm shrink toward zero is itself a legitimate convergence diagnostic used in real training loops.</p>"
},

{ topic:"prob", q:"Which of the following are correct examples of applying the addition rule and complement rule together in a practical ML-evaluation context?",
  options:[
    {t:"P(a random test example is misclassified by at least one of two independently-erring models) can be computed via 1 − P(neither model errs), using the complement rule", c:true},
    {t:"P(a prediction is either a false positive or a false negative) = P(false positive) + P(false negative), since these two error types are disjoint (a single prediction can't be both at once)", c:true},
    {t:"P(A ∪ B) can always be safely computed as P(A) + P(B) regardless of whether A and B overlap", c:false},
    {t:"These rules give exact answers only when the relevant events' probabilities are already known or reliably estimated from data — they don't manufacture correct probabilities out of bad estimates", c:true}
  ],
  exp:"<p>This closes the probability section by tying the addition/complement rules directly back to metrics an ML engineer computes daily: false-positive and false-negative are mutually exclusive outcomes for a single prediction (a given example is either one or the other, never both), so their probabilities add directly, while 'at least one of several independent things happens' is the textbook complement-rule shortcut. The caveat about needing good underlying probability estimates is a genuinely important, often-skipped point: these identities are mathematically exact given true probabilities, but in practice P(false positive) etc. are themselves estimated from a finite validation set and inherit that estimate's own uncertainty.</p>"
}];

QUESTIONS.forEach((q, i) => { q.id = 'q' + (i + 1); q._idx = i + 1; });
const groups: Record<string, typeof QUESTIONS> = {};
for (const q of QUESTIONS) {
  (groups[q.topic] = groups[q.topic] || []).push(q);
}
const sections = TOPIC_ORDER
  .map((key, i) => ({ key, index: i + 1, meta: (TOPIC_META as any)[key], qs: groups[key] || [] }))
  .filter((s) => s.qs.length > 0);
const totalQuestions = QUESTIONS.length;
---

<BaseLayout title="ML Concepts Quiz — Part 1" description="101 multi-select questions covering ML foundations through gradient descent and probability, with worked explanations for every option.">
  <div class="container quiz-page">
    <a class="backlink" href="/machine-learning/ml-concepts-revision-part-1/">&larr; Back to Part 1: Quickly revise ML concepts</a>

    <div class="qhero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Machine Learning &middot; Self-test</p>
      <h1>ML Concepts Quiz &mdash; Part 1</h1>
      <p class="tagline">{totalQuestions} multi-select questions covering everything in Part 1, from what ML even is through gradient descent and probability. Select any options you think are true, then check &mdash; each question reveals its own worked explanation.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="scoreboard">
      <div class="score-stat"><span class="n" id="stat-answered">0/{totalQuestions}</span><span class="l">Answered</span></div>
      <div class="score-stat"><span class="n" id="stat-correct">0</span><span class="l">Correct</span></div>
      <div class="score-bar-track"><div class="score-bar-fill" id="score-bar"></div></div>
      <button class="reset-btn" id="reset-btn" type="button">Reset progress</button>
    </div>

    <nav class="topicnav">
      {sections.map((s) => (
        <a href={`#sec-${s.key}`}>{s.index}. {s.meta.title}</a>
      ))}
    </nav>

    <main>
      {sections.map((s) => (
        <section class="topic-section">
          <div class="section-head" id={`sec-${s.key}`}>
            <p class="num">Section {s.index} &middot; {s.qs.length} questions</p>
            <h2>{s.meta.title}</h2>
            <p class="blurb">{s.meta.blurb}</p>
          </div>

          <div class="qlist">
            {s.qs.map((q) => (
              <div class="qcard" id={q.id}>
                <div class="qhead">
                  <span class="qnum">Q{q._idx}</span>
                  <span class="qtag">{s.meta.title}</span>
                </div>
                <p class="qtext">{q.q}</p>
                <p class="qhint">Select all statements you believe are true, then check your answer.</p>

                <ul class="options">
                  {q.options.map((opt: any, oi: number) => (
                    <li class="opt">
                      <input type="checkbox" id={`${q.id}-o${oi}`} data-correct={opt.c ? "1" : "0"} />
                      <label for={`${q.id}-o${oi}`} class="otext">{opt.t}</label>
                      <span class="mark"></span>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>

                <div class="qfoot">
                  <button type="button" class="check-btn">Check answer</button>
                  <span class="verdict"></span>
                </div>

                <div class="explain">
                  <span class="lbl">Explanation</span>
                  <div class="explain-body" set:html={q.exp}></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>
      ))}
    </main>

    <footer class="endnote">
      That's Part 1 &mdash; foundations, hypothesis spaces, bias/variance, VC-dimension, preprocessing, linear regression, gradient descent, and core probability. Part 2 will pick up Bayes decision theory and parameter estimation.
    </footer>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .quiz-page {
    padding-top: var(--space-5);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-6);
    --good: #1a7f4e;
    --good-soft: #e4f5ec;
    --good-line: #a9dcc2;
    --bad: #b8382f;
    --bad-soft: #fbeae8;
    --bad-line: #eab5af;
    --miss: #9a6b06;
    --miss-soft: #fbf1dc;
    --miss-line: #e9cd8e;
    --sunken: #f1efe9;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    .quiz-page {
      --good: #52d98f;
      --good-soft: #113523;
      --good-line: #205c3d;
      --bad: #ff7a70;
      --bad-soft: #3a1917;
      --bad-line: #6b302b;
      --miss: #e8b84b;
      --miss-soft: #362c10;
      --miss-line: #6b551f;
      --sunken: #17171c;
    }
  }

  .backlink {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }
  .backlink:hover { color: var(--color-accent); }

  .qhero { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
  }
  .qhero h1 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-2xl);
  }
  .tagline {
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--fs-base);
    max-width: 68ch;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .scoreboard {
    position: sticky;
    top: var(--space-2);
    z-index: 5;
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    align-items: center;
    background: var(--color-bg-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
    margin: var(--space-4) 0;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 10px rgba(20,15,50,0.06);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .score-stat { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1px; min-width: 60px; }
  .score-stat .n { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; font-weight: 600; font-size: var(--fs-lg); }
  .score-stat .l { font-size: 11px; color: var(--color-ink-muted); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; }
  .score-bar-track { flex: 1 1 140px; height: 6px; border-radius: 4px; background: var(--sunken); overflow: hidden; min-width: 90px; }
  .score-bar-fill { height: 100%; background: var(--color-accent); width: 0%; transition: width 0.25s ease; }
  .reset-btn {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    background: none;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 5px 10px;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .reset-btn:hover { color: var(--bad); border-color: var(--bad-line); }

  .topicnav {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-1);
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }
  .topicnav a {
    flex: none;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11.5px;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    background: var(--color-bg-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    padding: 6px 10px;
    border-radius: 999px;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .topicnav a:hover { color: var(--color-accent); border-color: var(--color-accent); }

  .topic-section { margin-bottom: var(--space-5); }
  .section-head {
    scroll-margin-top: var(--space-4);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    padding-top: var(--space-2);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  .section-head .num {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
  }
  .section-head h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-xl);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
  }
  .section-head .blurb { color: var(--color-ink-muted); font-size: var(--fs-sm); margin: 0; max-width: 60ch; }

  .qlist { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-3); }

  .qcard {
    background: var(--color-bg-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--space-4);
  }
  .qhead { display: flex; gap: var(--space-2); align-items: baseline; margin-bottom: var(--space-2); flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .qnum { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-accent); font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .qtag {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 10.5px;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }
  .qtext { font-size: var(--fs-base); font-weight: 500; margin: 0 0 4px; }
  .qhint { font-size: var(--fs-sm); color: var(--color-ink-muted); margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); }

  .options { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px; }
  .opt {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 10px;
    padding: 9px 10px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
    background: var(--color-bg);
    transition: border-color .12s ease, background .12s ease;
  }
  .opt:hover { border-color: var(--color-accent); }
  .opt input { margin-top: 3px; accent-color: var(--color-accent); flex: none; }
  .opt .otext { flex: 1; cursor: pointer; }
  .opt .mark { flex: none; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 12px; font-weight: 600; opacity: 0; }

  .qcard.checked .opt { cursor: default; }
  .qcard.checked .opt.sel-correct { background: var(--good-soft); border-color: var(--good-line); }
  .qcard.checked .opt.sel-correct .mark { opacity: 1; color: var(--good); }
  .qcard.checked .opt.sel-wrong { background: var(--bad-soft); border-color: var(--bad-line); }
  .qcard.checked .opt.sel-wrong .mark { opacity: 1; color: var(--bad); }
  .qcard.checked .opt.missed { background: var(--miss-soft); border-color: var(--miss-line); }
  .qcard.checked .opt.missed .mark { opacity: 1; color: var(--miss); }

  .qfoot { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-3); flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .check-btn {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    color: var(--color-accent-ink);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    border: none;
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .check-btn:hover { filter: brightness(1.08); }
  .check-btn.is-hidden { display: none; }
  .verdict { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.82rem; font-weight: 600; display: none; }
  .verdict.show { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 6px; }
  .verdict.pass { color: var(--good); }
  .verdict.fail { color: var(--bad); }

  .explain {
    display: none;
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    padding: var(--space-3) 14px;
    background: var(--sunken);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
    border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  .explain.show { display: block; }
  .explain .lbl {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 10.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 5px;
  }
  .explain-body :global(p) { margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .explain-body :global(p:last-child) { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .explain-body :global(code) {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    background: var(--color-accent-soft);
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 0.88em;
  }

  footer.endnote {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    padding-top: var(--space-4);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
  }

  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .qhead { flex-wrap: wrap; }
  }
</style>

<script>
  const STORAGE_KEY = "ml_quiz_part1_progress_v1";

  function loadProgress(): Record<string, boolean> {
    try {
      const raw = localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
      return raw ? JSON.parse(raw) : {};
    } catch (e) { return {}; }
  }
  function saveProgress(state: Record<string, boolean>) {
    try { localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, JSON.stringify(state)); } catch (e) {}
  }

  const progress = loadProgress();
  const cards = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".qcard"));
  const totalEl = document.getElementById("stat-answered");
  const correctEl = document.getElementById("stat-correct");
  const barEl = document.getElementById("score-bar") as HTMLElement | null;
  const total = cards.length;

  function updateScoreboard() {
    let answered = 0, correct = 0;
    for (const id in progress) {
      answered++;
      if (progress[id]) correct++;
    }
    if (totalEl) totalEl.textContent = answered + "/" + total;
    if (correctEl) correctEl.textContent = String(correct);
    if (barEl) barEl.style.width = (total ? Math.round((answered / total) * 100) : 0) + "%";
  }

  function checkCard(card: HTMLElement) {
    const opts = Array.from(card.querySelectorAll<HTMLLIElement>(".opt"));
    let allCorrectChosen = true;
    let anyWrongChosen = false;

    for (const li of opts) {
      const input = li.querySelector("input") as HTMLInputElement;
      const mark = li.querySelector(".mark") as HTMLElement;
      const isCorrect = input.dataset.correct === "1";
      const picked = input.checked;
      if (isCorrect && !picked) {
        li.classList.add("missed");
        mark.textContent = "✓ correct answer (missed)";
        allCorrectChosen = false;
      } else if (isCorrect && picked) {
        li.classList.add("sel-correct");
        mark.textContent = "✓ correct";
      } else if (!isCorrect && picked) {
        li.classList.add("sel-wrong");
        mark.textContent = "✗ incorrect";
        anyWrongChosen = true;
      }
      input.disabled = true;
    }

    const pass = allCorrectChosen && !anyWrongChosen;
    card.classList.add("checked");
    const explain = card.querySelector(".explain");
    if (explain) explain.classList.add("show");
    const verdict = card.querySelector(".verdict") as HTMLElement;
    if (verdict) {
      verdict.classList.add("show", pass ? "pass" : "fail");
      verdict.textContent = pass ? "✓ Correct" : "✗ Not quite — see explanation";
    }
    const btn = card.querySelector(".check-btn") as HTMLElement;
    if (btn) btn.classList.add("is-hidden");

    progress[card.id] = pass;
    saveProgress(progress);
    updateScoreboard();
  }

  for (const card of cards) {
    const btn = card.querySelector(".check-btn");
    if (btn) btn.addEventListener("click", () => checkCard(card));
  }

  const resetBtn = document.getElementById("reset-btn");
  if (resetBtn) {
    resetBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
      try { localStorage.removeItem(STORAGE_KEY); } catch (e) {}
      location.reload();
    });
  }

  updateScoreboard();
</script>
